<section id="premise">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="ve-label">Premise</p>
    <h2>The input is never clean. The system is built for that.</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <p>Studio work does not arrive as a tidy form. A client sends a poster link. A candidate sends a portfolio PDF inside a forwarded conversation. A teammate photographs a receipt. Someone records a voice note while walking. The original material is the only reliable source, so the assistant accepts it as it is and does the normalizing itself.</p>
      <p>The product rule is simple: <b>AI prepares, a person decides.</b> The bot may read, classify, extract, and draft. Writes that change the studio’s system of record stay explicit, reviewable, and reversible.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="note-box">
      <h3>What makes it a product, not a chatbot</h3>
      <p>Durable state in PostgreSQL, permission-gated topics, idempotent writes, background workers for slow media, 1 ledger for AI spend, and an admin panel that shows services, costs, and recoverable errors.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="topics">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="ve-label">Product surface</p>
    <h2>Nine topics are the product.</h2>
  </div>
  <figure>
    <div class="topic-map">
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>Calendar</h3><span class="chip ok">live</span></div>
        <p class="io">Text / voice / image / link → event draft → Google Calendar</p>
        <p>Extracts date, time, place, guests, and recurrence. Parses poster links through JSON-LD without spending AI tokens. Holds clarification context for up to three rounds.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>HR</h3><span class="chip ok">live</span></div>
        <p class="io">Text / forward / PDF / image → candidate card → HR Sheets</p>
        <p>Collects forwarded material into one batch, reads PDFs with Docling and OCR, deduplicates candidates, and writes one person to one primary profile sheet.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>Avatars</h3><span class="chip ok">live</span></div>
        <p class="io">Text / voice idea → adapted prompt → 1024×1024&nbsp;PNG</p>
        <p>Translates and adapts the idea through Surplus, generates a sticker through Replicate, and composites the exact SHUM background color #F3AC03.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>Budget</h3><span class="chip ok">live</span></div>
        <p class="io">Receipt / text / voice → expense or income draft → finance Sheets</p>
        <p>Handles monthly expenses, income history with the 8%&nbsp;AUSN tax, expected receipts, safe arithmetic formulas, and a knowledge base of recurring merchants.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>PR and marketing</h3><span class="chip ok">live</span></div>
        <p class="io">Link / screenshot / text → publication draft → PR Sheets</p>
        <p>Deduplicates by URL, updates rows in place, reads browser address bars from screenshots, and enriches links with SSRF-safe web fetching.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>Client base</h3><span class="chip ok">live</span></div>
        <p class="io">Text / link / media / PDF → company card → five client sheets</p>
        <p>Deduplicates by website and domain, accepts media and forwarded input, and adds human-only comments after a successful save.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>Personal data</h3><span class="chip warn">owner-only</span></div>
        <p class="io">Button → read-only document card → Telegram only</p>
        <p>Reads RU passports, foreign passports, and vehicles from a dedicated workbook. Data never reaches AI providers, logs, or PostgreSQL. Cards are formatted for one-tap copy.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>Enquiries</h3><span class="chip ok">live</span></div>
        <p class="io">Website form → minimized receipt → Telegram topic</p>
        <p>A protected ingress accepts enquiries from studio-shum.ru, uses a no-replay protocol, and anonymizes cards after 30&nbsp;days.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="topic">
        <div class="topic-head"><h3>Estimates</h3><span class="chip warn">reserved</span></div>
        <p class="io">Brief → estimate → estimate workbook</p>
        <p>The topic exists in the catalog, but the business slice is not built. It needs a separate owner-run OAuth bootstrap before file creation can work.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 2</span><span class="fig-text">Each topic ends in the system that already owns the data. The bot is an interface, not a second database.</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <details class="deep">
    <summary>Why topics instead of one chat</summary>
    <div class="deep-body">
      <p>Telegram topics give each function a stable routing key. The bot stores <code>chat_id + function_key → message_thread_id</code> in <code>bot_owned_topics</code> and resolves the function from the thread, never from the topic title or the first words of a message. If an unknown topic is used, the bot refuses to guess. If a permission was revoked, the bot sends an explicit refusal instead of falling back to a default function.</p>
      <p>This matters because the same bot serves several people with different access. The topic is the boundary between “a message” and “an operation with consequences”.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="lifecycle">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="ve-label">One request, end to end</p>
    <h2>The webhook is the only synchronous door.</h2>
  </div>
  <figure class="lifecycle-figure">
    <div class="lifecycle">
      <div class="stage-track" role="group" aria-label="Request lifecycle stages">
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="1" aria-pressed="true"><span class="n">01</span><strong>Update arrives</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="2" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">02</span><strong>Edge and budget</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="3" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">03</span><strong>Route and gate</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="4" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">04</span><strong>Normalize</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="5" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">05</span><strong>Enrich media</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="6" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">06</span><strong>Draft with AI</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="7" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">07</span><strong>Human decision</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="8" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">08</span><strong>Controlled write</strong></button>
        <button class="stage" type="button" data-stage="9" aria-pressed="false"><span class="n">09</span><strong>Record and observe</strong></button>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-copy" aria-live="polite">
        <p data-stage-copy="1">Telegram sends a signed POST to <code>/webhook/telegram</code>. The same bot token must not be used by local polling and the production webhook at the same time.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="2" hidden>Caddy terminates HTTPS and proxies the request. Its write timeout is 60&nbsp;seconds. The handler keeps a 5-second safety margin, so the application budget is 50&nbsp;seconds.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="3" hidden>The router reads <code>chat_id</code> and <code>message_thread_id</code>, finds the function, and checks <code>FunctionPermission</code>. Unknown topics and revoked users get an explicit refusal.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="4" hidden>The ingest layer normalizes text, captions, forwarded provenance, entities, hidden links, and attachment descriptors. Diagnostic logs stay metadata-only.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="5" hidden>Voice goes to Yandex SpeechKit. Images go to the Vision worker. PDFs go to Docling and OCR. Durable batches survive retries, restarts, and slow documents.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="6" hidden>The matching function turns evidence into a typed draft. Every real model call goes through the AI gateway and writes a usage event with tokens or audio seconds.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="7" hidden>A Telegram Rich Card shows the draft. The human can approve, correct, or cancel. HR and Budget add durable drafts, interaction-mode gates, and one-use tokens.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="8" hidden>On approval, Google Calendar or Sheets writes run in a worker thread. Idempotency, row fingerprints, and no-replay tokens prevent double saves after a repeated tap.</p>
        <p data-stage-copy="9" hidden>The journal records safe before and after values. Failures become deduplicated incidents. Spend lands in AI usage. The admin panel reads all of it without prompts or private content.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-controls">
        <button type="button" data-previous aria-label="Previous stage">←</button>
        <button type="button" class="replay" data-replay>Replay</button>
        <span class="stage-position" aria-hidden="true">1 / 9</span>
        <button type="button" data-next aria-label="Next stage">→</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 3</span><span class="fig-text">Slow work leaves the request immediately. If a handler exceeds 50&nbsp;seconds, the bot records an incident and returns HTTP 200 so Telegram does not retry in a storm.</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

