<section id="media">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="ve-label">Media</p>
    <h2>Three pipelines carry the material AI cannot read directly.</h2>
  </div>
  <figure>
    <div class="pipelines">
      <article class="pipeline">
        <h3>Voice and video notes</h3>
        <div class="pipe-steps">
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Telegram sends OggOpus for voice, MP4 for a video note.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>MP4 is converted to MP3 by FFmpeg through a short-lived seekable file in <code>/dev/shm</code>.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Yandex SpeechKit transcribes. Recognition routes by duration: ≤&nbsp;30&nbsp;s synchronous, longer asynchronous.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>The recognized text is injected into the topic as a normal text message.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="pipe-note">Header-only MP3 output is rejected. The temporary input is deleted in <code>finally</code>.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="pipeline">
        <h3>Images and screenshots</h3>
        <div class="pipe-steps">
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Images enter a durable media batch, not the webhook’s request path.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Safety checks and OCR run first. A VLM reads the visual content when needed.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Function adapters shape the result for Calendar, HR, PR, or Client Base.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>A single terminal notification resumes the waiting flow.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="pipe-note">The HR Vision adapter always drops the human-only <code>Comment</code> field.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="pipeline">
        <h3>PDF and documents</h3>
        <div class="pipe-steps">
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Eligible PDFs create attachment jobs and a continuation batch before any HR card is sent.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Docling extracts text. Image-heavy pages escalate to OCRmyPDF and Tesseract.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>Follow-up messages in the same topic append to the active batch instead of being discarded.</span></div>
          <div class="pipe-step"><span class="dot"></span><span>The resumed card sees the combined text, captions, attachments, and full extracted PDF text.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="pipe-note">Background timeout is 300&nbsp;s. Worker lease is <code>max(90, timeout × 2)</code>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 5</span><span class="fig-text">The slowest material never blocks the bot. Each pipeline stores enough state to finish after a restart.</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <details class="deep">
    <summary>Why the document path is the riskiest part</summary>
    <div class="deep-body">
      <p>A PDF is not a message. It can be 200 pages, scanned, rotated, or mostly images. It can arrive in five forwarded parts. The original Telegram file may keep the MP4 <code>moov</code> atom at the end, which makes naive streaming fail. OCR can take minutes. A worker can die mid-job.</p>
      <p>The system answers with durable jobs, a continuation batch, an adaptive lease, and fail-closed behavior. If the PDF times out, is cancelled, or cannot be parsed, the HR card is not sent. The bot does not invent a candidate from half a document.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

