<section id="reliability">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <p class="ve-label">Reliability</p>
    <h2>The hard part is not the model. It is the second attempt.</h2>
  </div>
  <figure>
    <div class="budget-frame">
      <svg class="ve-bars" viewBox="0 0 240 108" role="img" aria-label="The webhook budget is 50 seconds. Web fetch is 14 seconds. STT sync is 30 seconds. OCR gets 300 seconds in a background worker."><text y="12">Web fetch</text><rect x="84" y="5" width="4.67" height="14" rx="2"/><text x="96.67" y="12">14s</text><text y="40">STT sync</text><rect x="84" y="33" width="10" height="14" rx="2"/><text x="102" y="40">30s</text><text y="68">Webhook</text><rect class="hot" x="84" y="61" width="16.67" height="14" rx="2"/><text x="108.67" y="68">50s</text><text y="96">OCR</text><rect x="84" y="89" width="100" height="14" rx="2"/><text x="192" y="96">300s</text></svg>
      <div class="budget-notes">
        <p><b>Synchronous, 50&nbsp;s or less.</b> Web fetch, speech-to-text for short clips, and the webhook itself protect one shared budget. The bot must answer Telegram inside it.</p>
        <p><b>Background, 300&nbsp;s.</b> OCR is a separate worker with a separate lease. A long PDF can finish after the webhook has already returned.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 7</span><span class="fig-text">Most limits protect the 50-second synchronous webhook. OCR is allowed 300&nbsp;seconds only because it runs out of band.</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="ve-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Reliability safeguards comparison. Scroll horizontally to explore.">
    <p class="scroll-hint" aria-hidden="true">Swipe horizontally to compare →</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Failure mode</th><th>Mechanism that answers it</th><th>Visible result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Telegram retry storm</th><td>A 50&nbsp;s handler budget. On expiry: record <code>telegram_webhook:timeout:TimeoutError</code> and return HTTP 200. Five fast successful updates recover the incident.</td><td><span class="chip ok">self-healing</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Double tap on Save</th><td>Atomic claim with <code>with_for_update()</code>, version increment, one-use sibling tokens, and <code>reconciliation_required</code> after an ambiguous timeout.</td><td><span class="chip ok">no replay</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Google call blocks the bot</th><td>Synchronous Sheets and Calendar calls are offloaded to worker threads with <code>asyncio.to_thread</code> or <code>_run_blocking</code>.</td><td><span class="chip ok">no frozen loop</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Worker dies on a long OCR</th><td>Adaptive lease <code>max(90, timeout × 2)</code>, explicit state transitions, and a stale-claim sweep on the next loop.</td><td><span class="chip ok">durable job</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Duplicate candidate or client</th><td>Deduplication by phone, site, domain, or URL. Preview before write. HR writes one person to one primary sheet.</td><td><span class="chip ok">one row</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>WAF or DDoS-Guard tarpit</th><td>Chrome 142 headers, TCP/TLS connect cap, bounded fast retry, one 14&nbsp;s deadline per URL, 5&nbsp;MB cap.</td><td><span class="chip ok">bounded fetch</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>PII leaks into AI or logs</th><td>Personal Data is read-only, never sent to AI, never stored in PostgreSQL or incidents. Incidents carry metadata only. HR and Budget comments are human-only.</td><td><span class="chip ok">contained</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Restart during a write</th><td>Durable drafts, media batches, continuation batches, idempotency keys, and a startup recovery sweep for stale saving drafts.</td><td><span class="chip ok">recoverable</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <details class="deep">
    <summary>The exact constants</summary>
    <div class="deep-body">
      <div class="ve-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Reliability constants comparison. Scroll horizontally to explore.">
        <p class="scroll-hint" aria-hidden="true">Swipe horizontally to compare →</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Constant</th><th>Value</th><th>What it protects</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th><code>TELEGRAM_HANDLER_BUDGET_SECONDS</code></th><td class="num">50</td><td>The synchronous webhook response. Never applied to background tasks.</td></tr>
            <tr><th><code>ATTACHMENT_ENRICHMENT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS</code></th><td class="num">300</td><td>The OCR worker. Cutting it to 50 s would kill valid PDFs.</td></tr>
            <tr><th><code>WEB_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS</code></th><td class="num">14</td><td>One URL across HR, PR, Client Base, and Calendar. Validator ceiling is 18 s.</td></tr>
            <tr><th><code>PENDING_HR_BATCH_TTL_SECONDS</code></th><td class="num">1 800</td><td>The HR preview confirmation token: 30&nbsp;minutes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Saving-draft lease</th><td class="num">300</td><td>Recovery of writes stuck in <code>saving</code> for 5&nbsp;minutes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Vision work directory</th><td>tmpfs</td><td><code>/tmp/shum-vision-work</code>, outside the persistent volume.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small">One number is deliberately missing: there is no single “AI timeout” for everything. Each provider and each worker has its own contract because text, speech, images, and OCR fail in different ways.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

